  [1mMy recommendation is the first one.[0m For a learning project it's the highest-value option by a wide margin — the
  switcher is maybe thirty lines, and in exchange you get to build the actual interesting mechanic: an event carries an
  actor, subscribers are resolved from a relationship, and the actor is excluded from their own notifications. That's
  the part worth understanding. The other two options let you skip it.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where do the changes come from, given there's only one browser? → Simulated users + switcher (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the other half of "tasks they care about": what creates that relationship?

  This determines the data model — whether a task carries a watcher list, or whether "caring" is derived from fields the
  task already has. Deriving it is free but rigid; an explicit list is a little more code but lets someone follow a
  task they have no formal role in. GitHub-style auto-watch (touch it and you're subscribed) is the interesting middle:
  derived by default, explicitly overridable.

  I'd lean toward [1massignee + creator, plus an explicit watch toggle[0m — the derived roles mean notifications work
  immediately with zero setup, and the toggle gives you something to click to prove subscription is real and not just a
  hardcoded rule.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Subscription [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhat makes a task one you "care about"?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mRoles + watch toggle (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mAssignee and creator are subscribed automatically; anyone can also explicitly watch/unwatch any task. Works with [39m
     [38;5;246mzero setup, but the toggle makes subscription visible and testable.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Assignee only
     [38;5;246mSimplest possible rule: you're notified about tasks assigned to you. No watcher list in the data model. Least code,
[39m     [38;5;246mleast to demonstrate.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Explicit watch only
     [38;5;246mNothing is automatic; you subscribe to tasks deliberately. Cleanest concept, but the app feels dead until you've [39m
     [38;5;246mclicked watch on things.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m Auto-watch on any interaction
     [38;5;246mGitHub-style: create, comment on, or edit a task and you're subscribed until you mute it. Most realistic, most [39m
     [38;5;246mrules to get right.[39m
